Welcome to the "Rubin Report."

00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:08.268
- Nice to see ya,
how's it going?

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:09.469
- It is going well.

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:12.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
I feel this is overdue
for many reasons,

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:15.075
because as a guy that
came from the left

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:17.844 align:start size:97% position:3%
and started saying these
people are no longer liberal,

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
and what's this
social justice thing

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:22.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
and intersectionality
and all of it,

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:26.986
you were sort of the
king or the queen.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:27.921
- Whichever word,
don't misgender me,

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:28.822
but it's one or the other, yeah.

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.524
(Dave laughing)

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:34.727 line:0%
- Of this, you were the king
or the queen of this thing.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:37.063 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- I mean, I accused of often
going over to the right,

00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:38.465 line:0%
which isn't really the case.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:41.134 align:start size:84% position:16%
I come from a
leftist background,

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:43.670
but I think that conservatives
are right about some things,

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.372 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think left-wing people are
right about other things.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:49.542
I think if you subscribe
to an ideology wholesale

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:50.810
and you just say, "That's me,

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.112
"I gonna go agree
with those points,"

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:53.880
then you're not really
thinking for yourself,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.148
and that scares me a little bit,

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:56.649
(chuckles) so I don't do that.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:58.118 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, all right, well,
we're gonna have to clean up

00:01:58.118 --> 00:01:59.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
some of that in the
course of this next hour.

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:00.653
Now, I'm a little
confused though,

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:04.691
because when we
were pitched you,

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:05.959
this is your book, by the way.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.193
- [Andrew] Well, it's
Titania's book, yeah.

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:09.095
- Well, so this
is the confusion.

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:11.998
So I thought that we were
having Titania McGrath

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:13.466 align:start size:97% position:3%
on the show.
- Yeah, she's not available.

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.001
- And it's called "Woke,"

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:17.504
and it's a Guide
to Social Justice,

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:19.239
and I thought we were
having Ms. McGrath.

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:22.108 align:start size:97% position:3%
And yet here we end up with
you, so I'm a little confused.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.544 align:start size:84% position:16%
Can you possibly
dissect this for me?

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.012
- Well, she's out and about,

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:27.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
she's doing her
protesting, I guess.

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:28.982
(Dave laughing)

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.650 align:start size:97% position:3%
She's probably gluing herself
to someone's front door

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.453 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
who's considered a negative
influence on society.

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:34.687 line:0%
You know, that's
her kind of thing.

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.589 line:0%
But actually, the truth
is she doesn't exist.

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.725 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
I created her.
- You're blowing my mind, man.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:41.027 line:0%
- I know, it's not a
revelation, really.

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:44.664
It was April 2018, and I thought

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:47.200
I'm gonna create
a fake character

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.302 align:start size:84% position:16%
to satirize the social
justice movement,

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.771
well, the excesses of the
social justice movement.

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:56.042
I wanted to do this sort of
slam poet, very pretentious,

00:02:56.042 --> 00:03:00.947 align:start size:97% position:3%
young, very narcissistic,
kind of very privileged person

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:03.783
who wants to nevertheless
say that she's oppressed,

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:05.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
so reveling in
oppression and victimhood

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:07.120
and all of that stuff.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, this is quite a
recognizable type of person.

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:11.891
- Yes, I've seen one or two of
these people on the Twitter.

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:14.093
- And they're kind of funny,
because there's a lot of them,

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:15.562
particularly in the UK,

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:17.330 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's a lot of these sort
of left-leaning people.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.032 align:start size:94% position:6%
They tend to write for
things like "The Guardian"

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:22.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
and "The Independent,"
and they're very

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:23.736
all about their victim status,

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:26.005 align:start position:0%
but they come from independently
millionaire backgrounds,

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:27.240
and they don't see
that that's funny.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.274
I find that hilarious.

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:29.209
(Dave laughing)

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:30.443
And of course, they are funny,

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:31.978 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the other thing
about them, of course,

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:34.113
is they don't wanna sit and
debate and reason and talk,

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:35.582 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so therefore, the
best thing to do I think

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:37.083
is mock them and
poke fun of them.

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:39.185
And the hope was
that if I presented

00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:42.555
their own failings to them
through this caricature,

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:44.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
who obviously says
things that are beyond

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.326
what most normal people say,

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:48.695
it is a character,
it is an extreme,

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:52.065
but I thought it might maybe
start some kind of process

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:54.200
of self-reflection potentially.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.902
It doesn't, it just seems
to have got them angry.

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.503
- Well, it's funny,
because when I see

00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:00.273 align:start size:94% position:6%
Titania's Twitter account,
when I first saw it,

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:02.842
so this is now a year
and a half ago or so,

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:05.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's hard to tell
what's satire anymore,

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:10.250
because the things that you
or she is tweeting about,

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
they sound very
much like the stuff

00:04:12.685 --> 00:04:14.220
that we're constantly thrown at

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.722
by mainstream media actually,

00:04:15.722 --> 00:04:18.024 align:start size:94% position:6%
even though you're
trying to be over the top

00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:19.626 align:start size:81% position:19%
and quite absurd.
- Oh, yeah.

00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:21.794
Well, it catches up.
- Yeah (laughing).

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:22.929
- So I'm doing my best.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.498
I'll give you an
example of this.

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:28.101
So I sent a tweet, it was
last November I think,

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:30.503
about, you know the
film "Mary Poppins."

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:31.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right.
- So "Mary Poppins,"

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:32.739 align:start size:97% position:3%
Julie Andrews.
- I've seen it once or twice.

00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:34.240
- You've seen it, right.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.709
So she ends up covered with
chimney soot on her face.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:38.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
So Titania tweeted saying
this was blackface,

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.346 align:start position:0%
with there was a picture of
Julie Andrews with chimney soot.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:41.814
This is blackface,
it's disgusting.

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:44.083 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then five months
later, the "New York Times"

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:46.953
ran an article saying
exactly that, right?

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:48.488
So I predicted it,
and it's almost like,

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:50.089 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm not saying I've got
magical powers or anything,

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:53.626 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it is that
thing how can you...

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:55.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
When real life is catching
up with you so much,

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
there's virtually nothing
that I could tweet

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.399
that eventually won't happen.

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:02.101 align:start size:84% position:16%
When I did a joke
tweet about the queen,

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:03.870
it was the queen's
speech at Christmas,

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.305
and I was saying
that she should,

00:05:05.305 --> 00:05:06.873
listing the things
she should have said.

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:07.874
And I was saying that
she should have said

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:09.575
what about the new royal baby

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.444
and how it should be
raised as gender fluid.

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:12.578
(Dave laughing)

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:13.646 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then lo and
behold, that was raised

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:15.348
by Meghan a few months later.

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:19.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
On one side, it's funny, and
then it also depresses me

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:20.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
a little bit that
these things happen.

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:24.891
- Okay, so before we get too
far into the psychology of it,

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:26.392 align:start size:97% position:3%
can you just explain a little
bit of your background?

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:29.562
As you said, you grew
up kinda lefty, comedy,

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.698
all of that stuff, just for
the people that don't know.

00:05:31.698 --> 00:05:34.200
- Okay, yeah, I've been
a writer for a long time.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.436
Well, I started out in academia

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:38.971
and I wanted to get out
of that pretty quickly.

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:39.906
- [Dave] What were you teaching?

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:41.407
- I was teaching Shakespeare,

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.343
I was teaching the Shakespeare
module at Oxford University.

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:46.179 align:start position:0%
- Was there a connection
between the social justice thing

00:05:46.179 --> 00:05:47.680 align:start size:81% position:19%
and wanting to get
outta teaching?

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.117
- I was in Wadham College at
Oxford, which is the most,

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:54.120
it's known for being the
most woke Oxford college

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.357
before the word woke was even
being used by most people.

00:05:57.357 --> 00:05:58.558
It wasn't connected, no.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.262
I just thought I'm stuck
in libraries all the time.

00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:04.430
It's quite depressing,

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.232 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I thought I'd
better get out of that.

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:08.701 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I went out, I started
writing comedy plays

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:11.971
and doing standup in London,
and then I ran out of money

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:13.306
and I had nothing,
so I was teaching,

00:06:13.306 --> 00:06:15.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
so I took up secondary
school teaching.

00:06:15.608 --> 00:06:17.076 align:start size:81% position:19%
I did that for
about seven years.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:18.311
And all the while
I was doing standup

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:19.512
and all the while
I was writing plays

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:20.446
and writing stuff like that.

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:21.981
So that's my sort of background,

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:24.350
and I've been on the comedy
circuit for a long time now,

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:25.885
about 15 years now.

00:06:25.885 --> 00:06:28.488
- How woke is Oxford generally?

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:30.356
I was there a couple of times

00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:31.290
when I was on tour with Jordan,

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:32.525
and I spoke at Oxford Union,

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:33.526
and I thought the questions

00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:35.595
were really rich
and interesting,

00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:38.164
but then you could feel
that there is this strain

00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:41.267
of something getting into
the oldest, greatest place

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.703 align:start size:97% position:3%
of higher learning, like
this scary thing getting in.

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:47.306 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, there's
evidence to suggest

00:06:47.306 --> 00:06:49.609 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the most
privileged students,

00:06:49.609 --> 00:06:51.611
the most privileged people,
the richest, in other words,

00:06:51.611 --> 00:06:54.514 align:start position:0%
are far more likely to be
social justice activists, right?

00:06:54.514 --> 00:06:59.485 align:start size:88% position:13%
And "Spiked" online
does a survey of
universities every year

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:01.521
and every single year, it's
determined that the ones

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:03.022
that the are the worst offenders

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:04.257
when it comes to censorship,
when it comes to free speech,

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:06.025 align:start size:88% position:13%
tend to be from the kids
who are the poshest,

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:08.294
who have the most stuff, right?

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:09.529
So it's not really surprising

00:07:09.529 --> 00:07:12.064
that you get the woke
elements at Oxford.

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:13.866
But as a kind of caveat
to that, what I would say

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:15.935 align:start size:97% position:3%
is I do talks at universities
around the country,

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:18.638
and what I've noticed is
the kids are often great

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:20.106 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they're often not
these sort of snowflakes

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:21.808
that people make them
out to be, they're not.

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:23.042
They wanna be challenged.

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:24.510
A number of the people
that I've spoken to,

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.479 align:start size:97% position:3%
they wanna be challenged, they
wanna hear the other side,

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:27.980
even if they don't
necessarily agree.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.383
It's generally the
academics, right?

00:07:30.383 --> 00:07:31.918
I did a talk at a
university recently

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:34.487
where the students were great,
they wanted to be challenged,

00:07:34.487 --> 00:07:36.689
but then the politics department

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:38.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
refused to publicize
my appearance,

00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:41.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they said any
talk that was antithetical

00:07:41.694 --> 00:07:44.297 align:start size:97% position:3%
to woke culture was against
their diversity policy.

00:07:44.297 --> 00:07:46.332
So they boycotted it completely.

00:07:46.332 --> 00:07:49.602 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I often find it's my
generation that are the worst

00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:51.037
when it comes to this stuff.

00:07:51.037 --> 00:07:52.472 align:start size:81% position:19%
- How old are you?
- I'm 41.

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:54.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
- If you don't mind me asking.
- No, it's fine.

00:07:54.006 --> 00:07:57.376
- You're a Gen Xer, like me.
- I'm a Gen Xer, yeah, I am.

00:07:57.376 --> 00:07:59.312
- It's so interesting,
'cause I agree with that,

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:01.747
even though we see the
snowflakes at colleges

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:04.350
and the screaming people and
the memes of all that stuff,

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.453
that it does seem to really
come from the academic layer.

00:08:07.453 --> 00:08:10.156
I was giving a talk about
six months ago at a school,

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:11.757
and a guy came up to me after.

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.759 align:start size:88% position:13%
He was older than a
average college student,

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:14.827
and he told me that
he was a veteran

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:16.462
and he started college late.

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:19.565
He said that he was
in a philosophy class
earlier in the day

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:22.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the philosophy
professor instructed them

00:08:22.869 --> 00:08:26.572 align:start size:84% position:16%
not to go to Dave
Rubin's alt-right talk

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:28.074 align:start size:72% position:28%
that evening.
- Wow.

00:08:28.074 --> 00:08:29.575
- And he said, "I'm a little
older than these students,

00:08:29.575 --> 00:08:31.077
"so I didn't want to
embarrass the teacher

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:32.845 align:start size:94% position:6%
"in front of the class,
so I went up to him after,

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.647
"and I said, 'Can you tell me

00:08:34.647 --> 00:08:38.017 align:start size:94% position:6%
"'what alt-right opinions
Dave Rubin holds?'

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:39.285 align:start size:81% position:19%
"And the professor
basically said,

00:08:39.285 --> 00:08:41.420
"'Well, I don't know,
but I've read that.'"

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:44.090
And he was like, "I tried to
get the professor to come here,

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:45.725 align:start position:0%
"but then of course he wouldn't
come," and the rest of it.

00:08:45.725 --> 00:08:49.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
But the idea that they're
just being hit with this stuff

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:50.796 align:start size:84% position:16%
when they're the
most impressionable.

00:08:50.796 --> 00:08:54.500
- Exactly, but I think
it takes a certain degree

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.202
of education to say
things like that,

00:08:56.202 --> 00:08:57.670
because it's stupidity.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:08:59.939 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's a kind of learned
stupidity, which is really...

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:01.440
It's really odd, isn't it?

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.042
And I think it's because
when I was at university,

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:04.610
there was all this kind
of post-structuralism,

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:06.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
there was all
these kind of folks

00:09:06.078 --> 00:09:08.114 align:start size:97% position:3%
from Derrida, Foucault,
and all that kind of thing,

00:09:08.114 --> 00:09:10.249 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it was sort of dying
off a few years ago,

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:12.485 align:start position:0%
and now that's gone mainstream,
and I don't know why.

00:09:12.485 --> 00:09:14.020
I find that really weird.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.955
- Do you have suspicions as
to why it's gone mainstream,

00:09:15.955 --> 00:09:17.490
any theories?

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.959
- I don't know why, and I'm
often asking this question.

00:09:19.959 --> 00:09:21.427
I do want to know what it is.

00:09:22.862 --> 00:09:24.230
I can't fathom it, actually.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:25.464
I don't know why.

00:09:25.464 --> 00:09:27.500
It involves a kind of
substitution of slogans

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:28.968
for a lack of thought.

00:09:28.968 --> 00:09:31.771
So you get these phrases,
like lived experience,

00:09:31.771 --> 00:09:33.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
or microaggressions.
- All the phrases

00:09:33.205 --> 00:09:34.874 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are right here.
- Right, yeah.

00:09:34.874 --> 00:09:38.477 align:start size:97% position:3%
So what this means is,
when you discuss this stuff

00:09:38.477 --> 00:09:41.380
with a social justice activist,

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:42.915 align:start size:88% position:13%
when I talk to a social
justice activist,

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:45.418
I know what their opinion is
about absolutely everything.

00:09:45.418 --> 00:09:46.886 align:start size:81% position:19%
As soon as they've
said one thing,

00:09:46.886 --> 00:09:49.789 align:start position:0%
they're just repeating a script,
and that to me is really...

00:09:49.789 --> 00:09:51.490
That's not freedom of thought.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.326
That's an ideological adherence.

00:09:53.326 --> 00:09:54.894
And they use these phrases,

00:09:54.894 --> 00:09:56.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then when you challenge
them on the phrases,

00:09:56.395 --> 00:09:57.563 align:start size:84% position:16%
they don't know what
to do with that.

00:09:57.563 --> 00:09:59.065 align:start size:84% position:16%
To them, lived
experience is just,

00:09:59.065 --> 00:10:01.100
well, that ends the
discussion, right?

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.368
Well, actually,
lived experience,

00:10:02.368 --> 00:10:03.703
we used to call that
anecdotal evidence.

00:10:03.703 --> 00:10:04.937
(Dave laughing)

00:10:04.937 --> 00:10:06.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we used to dismiss it.
- Right.

00:10:06.472 --> 00:10:08.941
- But now actually it has
some kind of weird weight.

00:10:08.941 --> 00:10:10.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's that you come up with
these sort of phrases,

00:10:10.543 --> 00:10:11.944
these sort of phrases that have

00:10:11.944 --> 00:10:13.746 align:start size:84% position:16%
this kind of academic
sound about them,

00:10:13.746 --> 00:10:15.748 align:start size:88% position:13%
this jargon, this
intersectional quality,

00:10:15.748 --> 00:10:18.684 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's a way of giving
weight or ostensible weight

00:10:18.684 --> 00:10:20.586
to very flimsy ideas,
that's what it is.

00:10:20.586 --> 00:10:22.555
So it's like a kind of
substitute for thought.

00:10:22.555 --> 00:10:25.224
- So before we go too far
into those flimsy ideas,

00:10:25.224 --> 00:10:26.592
and I've listed all the words

00:10:26.592 --> 00:10:28.094
that you've got
on the book here,

00:10:28.094 --> 00:10:29.095
and I wanna get your take
on all of these things.

00:10:29.095 --> 00:10:30.029
- Sure.

00:10:30.029 --> 00:10:32.131
- But just for the few people

00:10:32.131 --> 00:10:33.265
that might be watching that go,

00:10:33.265 --> 00:10:35.468 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Oh, Rubin's got
another right-winger on

00:10:35.468 --> 00:10:38.070
"to talk about how evil the
social justice words are,"

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:41.240 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you say you come
sort of the left,

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:45.177 align:start size:97% position:3%
from a UK perspective, what
does that actually mean

00:10:45.177 --> 00:10:46.846
and maybe how is that
a little different

00:10:46.846 --> 00:10:48.514
than the American perspective?

00:10:48.514 --> 00:10:50.583 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, liberal means
something different in the UK.

00:10:50.583 --> 00:10:53.653
I mean, bottom line, I guess
where I fall on the spectrum,

00:10:53.653 --> 00:10:54.854
well firstly, what I should say,

00:10:54.854 --> 00:10:55.788 align:start size:84% position:16%
is I don't think
there's anything wrong

00:10:55.788 --> 00:10:57.089
with being conservative.

00:10:57.089 --> 00:10:58.391
It's when people say,
"I'm a conservative,"

00:10:58.391 --> 00:11:00.092
I'm like, "Well, so
what, that's fine."

00:11:00.092 --> 00:11:03.429
I don't happen to be, but I
don't see an issue if I were.

00:11:03.429 --> 00:11:05.564 align:start size:97% position:3%
If I were, I would just own
the label, it's not a problem.

00:11:05.564 --> 00:11:07.767 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It took me a long time
to get to that part.

00:11:07.767 --> 00:11:09.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, exactly.
- But you know what it is?

00:11:09.035 --> 00:11:10.770
Partly it's because
first they call you

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:12.271 align:start size:97% position:3%
a series of other much worse.
- Sure.

00:11:12.271 --> 00:11:14.273 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So they call you
far-right and alt-right,

00:11:14.273 --> 00:11:16.409 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then when the
average person realizes

00:11:16.409 --> 00:11:18.511 align:start size:97% position:3%
that doesn't stick, then
they just throw conservative,

00:11:18.511 --> 00:11:19.445
and then at that point,
it's like, all right,

00:11:19.445 --> 00:11:20.813
you wanna call me conservative?

00:11:20.813 --> 00:11:22.048
- Well, they throw those words

00:11:22.048 --> 00:11:23.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause they don't
know what they mean.

00:11:23.282 --> 00:11:24.216
I mean, alt-right, it's
really easy to check.

00:11:24.216 --> 00:11:25.951
Alt-right is white nationalist,

00:11:25.951 --> 00:11:27.720
and you can't get away
from those connotations.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.122
- So you're not a
white nationalist?

00:11:30.122 --> 00:11:32.425
- (laughing) No, so you
can tick that one off.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:36.495 align:start size:88% position:13%
So if I was to say
where am I politically,

00:11:36.495 --> 00:11:39.231 line:0%
it's somewhere between what
I would say social liberalism

00:11:39.231 --> 00:11:42.668 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
and socialism, somewhere
dancing around that,

00:11:42.668 --> 00:11:44.470 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
but I'm quite
culturally conservative,

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:46.205 line:0%
so there's elements
of that as well.

00:11:46.205 --> 00:11:49.442
But I guess I, you know, I
believe in the welfare state,

00:11:49.442 --> 00:11:51.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
I believe in
proportionate taxation

00:11:51.177 --> 00:11:52.778
for people who are earning more,

00:11:52.778 --> 00:11:54.280
not to excess, but
just enough to--

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:55.548
- So you're definitely
more left-wing than me.

00:11:55.548 --> 00:11:57.183 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right, sure.
- I'll give it to you.

00:11:57.183 --> 00:11:59.952 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So kinda just to curb
the excesses of capitalism,

00:11:59.952 --> 00:12:02.588 align:start size:81% position:19%
but not to get rid
of it entirely.

00:12:03.689 --> 00:12:06.992 align:start size:84% position:16%
I believe in equality
of opportunity.

00:12:06.992 --> 00:12:10.696 align:start size:88% position:13%
I believe in equal
rights and human rights.

00:12:10.696 --> 00:12:12.231
So all of those sort of things,

00:12:12.231 --> 00:12:16.335
which I think on the whole put
me in that left-wing bracket.

00:12:16.335 --> 00:12:18.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm not instinctively
nationalistic,

00:12:18.604 --> 00:12:20.372 align:start size:84% position:16%
although I understand
why people are.

00:12:20.372 --> 00:12:21.807
I just don't have that in me,

00:12:21.807 --> 00:12:23.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I don't think it's
a poisonous thing.

00:12:23.242 --> 00:12:26.345 align:start position:0%
- So is the problem for someone
that holds those sorta...

00:12:26.345 --> 00:12:27.580
What that sounds to me

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.081
is sort of like an
old-school Democrat.

00:12:29.081 --> 00:12:32.318
You want some social programs
and curb what you might see

00:12:32.318 --> 00:12:33.853
are the excesses of
capitalism, okay.

00:12:33.853 --> 00:12:35.287
- But I also want to curb
the excesses of the state,

00:12:35.287 --> 00:12:36.522
like I don't trust the state.

00:12:36.522 --> 00:12:38.224
- Right, so that's
sort of interesting,

00:12:38.224 --> 00:12:39.725
'cause it's sorta like,

00:12:39.725 --> 00:12:41.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
that to me is what we would
call here a blue-dog Democrat,

00:12:41.694 --> 00:12:43.896
that you don't want the
state to do everything.

00:12:43.896 --> 00:12:45.397 align:start size:88% position:13%
We don't really have
those Democrats anymore.

00:12:45.397 --> 00:12:47.800 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I want the state to stay
out of people's private lives.

00:12:49.268 --> 00:12:50.770 align:start size:81% position:19%
In the UK, they've
already proven

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:53.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they can't be trusted
on all sort of issues.

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:55.074
The fact that they're
criminalizing people

00:12:55.074 --> 00:12:56.475 align:start size:84% position:16%
who tell certain
jokes, for instance,

00:12:56.475 --> 00:12:57.777 align:start size:88% position:13%
the fact that they
don't see the difference

00:12:57.777 --> 00:13:00.212
between a joke and
the literal truth,

00:13:00.212 --> 00:13:02.314 align:start size:97% position:3%
that means that to me, I don't
want them getting involved

00:13:02.314 --> 00:13:04.950
in curbing people's speech,
and I think that the free...

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:06.519
I mean, you've got First
Amendment, you're okay.

00:13:06.519 --> 00:13:08.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, for now, for now.
- Sort of, for now.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:12.191 align:start position:0%
We don't have that, and we
have an unwritten constitution,

00:13:12.191 --> 00:13:14.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
which means it's constantly
being reinterpreted,

00:13:14.026 --> 00:13:15.561
and sometimes quite badly.

00:13:15.561 --> 00:13:18.230
- So as a free speech guy
and a guy that's out there

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:21.033 align:start size:84% position:16%
doing pretty
controversial stuff,

00:13:21.033 --> 00:13:22.568
how cognizant are you aware

00:13:22.568 --> 00:13:24.937
that you don't have
the First Amendment?

00:13:24.937 --> 00:13:28.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
So comedians here, you
can say whatever you want.

00:13:28.073 --> 00:13:30.976 align:start size:88% position:13%
Every time Trump tweets,
these edgy comedians,

00:13:31.844 --> 00:13:33.946
they tell him to go kiss off

00:13:33.946 --> 00:13:35.214
or whatever they say to him.
- Yeah, yeah, they can say

00:13:35.214 --> 00:13:36.448
whatever they want, yeah.

00:13:36.448 --> 00:13:37.983
And Trump doesn't
send the police,

00:13:37.983 --> 00:13:41.320
the Gestapo doesn't knock on
the door, you're good to go.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.989
But in the UK, you don't
have certain protections.

00:13:43.989 --> 00:13:46.192
- Okay, so it doesn't
affect me so much.

00:13:46.192 --> 00:13:48.928 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, I've never made the claim
that I'm being silenced.

00:13:48.928 --> 00:13:50.329 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm actually in a really
privileged position.

00:13:50.329 --> 00:13:53.299
I don't have a boss, I
can say whatever I want,

00:13:53.299 --> 00:13:55.768
I'm a comedian and a writer,
so I do say whatever I want.

00:13:55.768 --> 00:13:58.237
I've never felt that
I'm being censored.

00:13:58.237 --> 00:14:00.906 align:start size:97% position:3%
The concern is that actually
the people who do...

00:14:00.906 --> 00:14:03.275
I mean, look, say if
I was still a teacher,

00:14:03.275 --> 00:14:05.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
could I send the tweets
that I send at the moment?

00:14:05.477 --> 00:14:07.279
Absolutely not, I would
get in a lot of trouble.

00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:09.982
I know people who have
got in trouble at work

00:14:09.982 --> 00:14:11.317
because of jokes they've made

00:14:11.317 --> 00:14:13.652
or things that have
been misconstrued.

00:14:13.652 --> 00:14:15.087 align:start size:81% position:19%
A lot of people
who live in the UK

00:14:15.087 --> 00:14:18.057 align:start size:97% position:3%
are now talking to their
friends at work over WhatsApp,

00:14:18.057 --> 00:14:19.625
which means that their
jokes get encrypted,

00:14:19.625 --> 00:14:20.860
right?
- Mm-hmm.

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:22.061
- So that's really
weird, isn't it,

00:14:22.061 --> 00:14:23.529 align:start size:84% position:16%
the idea that you must
encrypt your jokes

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:25.698 align:start size:97% position:3%
in case they're overheard.
- Yeah, encrypted by a company

00:14:25.698 --> 00:14:27.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's owned by
Facebook, brilliant.

00:14:27.433 --> 00:14:28.267 align:start size:84% position:16%
Brilliant.
- Right, there we go.

00:14:28.267 --> 00:14:29.735
Yeah, that's great.

00:14:29.735 --> 00:14:32.471 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I mean, on the whole, I
think we have do have freedom.

00:14:32.471 --> 00:14:34.240
I don't think there's a big
freedom of speech crisis.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:37.109
What I mean is, you have
to be vigilant about it.

00:14:37.109 --> 00:14:38.711
You have to be careful
and you have to stand up.

00:14:38.711 --> 00:14:40.479 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we've got a situation
where the police are,

00:14:40.479 --> 00:14:44.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
there was a guy in Humberside
who retweeted a poem

00:14:44.717 --> 00:14:46.385
which was deemed
to be transphobic,

00:14:46.385 --> 00:14:48.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then the police
phoned him up and said,

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:49.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
"We need to check your
thinking on this."

00:14:49.889 --> 00:14:51.323 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Jesus.
- And he said,

00:14:51.323 --> 00:14:52.558
"Well, what's my crime?"

00:14:52.558 --> 00:14:53.792
And they said, and I'm
not kidding, they said,

00:14:53.792 --> 00:14:56.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
"This is a non-crime
hate incident."

00:14:56.595 --> 00:14:59.932
And that's standard police
practice, so that's a worry.

00:14:59.932 --> 00:15:02.534
- Well, so every few weeks,
I see one of those tweets.

00:15:02.534 --> 00:15:04.536
It's usually from the
Sussex police, right?

00:15:04.536 --> 00:15:07.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that who it is?
- Oh, it's all over the...

00:15:07.072 --> 00:15:08.240
- It's West Yorkshire police,

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:09.341 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's been the Glasgow
police as well.

00:15:09.341 --> 00:15:10.509
It's various police departments.

00:15:10.509 --> 00:15:13.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
- They literally
tweet out things like

00:15:13.078 --> 00:15:16.048
if someone that you
know said something

00:15:16.048 --> 00:15:17.716
that you might have
taken offense to,

00:15:17.716 --> 00:15:19.852
please let us know.
- Right, exactly.

00:15:19.852 --> 00:15:22.321
- I mean, how much more
Orwellian can you get?

00:15:22.321 --> 00:15:23.555 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, and you know,
whenever you use that word,

00:15:23.555 --> 00:15:25.457
Orwellian, people say
you're being cliche,

00:15:25.457 --> 00:15:26.725 align:start size:81% position:19%
but there is no
other word for it.

00:15:26.725 --> 00:15:28.427
What else are you gonna call it?

00:15:28.427 --> 00:15:29.728
That's exactly what it is.

00:15:31.196 --> 00:15:36.068
This isn't to the point of a
kind of authoritarian state,

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:37.870
but when you see these things,

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:39.305
you have to stand up and say,

00:15:39.305 --> 00:15:40.773 align:start size:97% position:3%
"No, we can't have this,
we've gotta get rid of this."

00:15:40.773 --> 00:15:42.708 align:start size:97% position:3%
And if you don't, that's where
the slippery slope begins.

00:15:42.708 --> 00:15:43.709 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, do you
think it's partially

00:15:43.709 --> 00:15:45.411
that people just don't stand up?

00:15:45.411 --> 00:15:47.513
So, for example, you keep
doing what you're doing.

00:15:47.513 --> 00:15:49.548 align:start size:84% position:16%
We know Douglas
Murray and Peter Lloyd

00:15:49.548 --> 00:15:52.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
and Paul Joseph Watson, I
mean, there's plenty of people

00:15:52.217 --> 00:15:54.620 align:start position:0%
all over the political spectrum
that do stand up to it.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:55.854
They haven't grabbed people

00:15:55.854 --> 00:15:57.356
and brought them
to the gulags yet,

00:15:57.356 --> 00:15:59.625
but it's like we just need
more people to stand up,

00:15:59.625 --> 00:16:02.161
and then they'll kinda
paper tiger disappear.

00:16:02.161 --> 00:16:04.029
- I think the trick is
you've gotta stand up

00:16:04.029 --> 00:16:05.331
for people you don't like.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.002
I understand why people
don't wanna stand up

00:16:10.002 --> 00:16:11.236
for the right thing.

00:16:11.236 --> 00:16:12.738
There's a thing called
preference falsification

00:16:12.738 --> 00:16:16.141
where you say what you think
is going to be best received,

00:16:16.141 --> 00:16:17.309
as opposed to what
you actually think.

00:16:17.309 --> 00:16:19.144
And I understand that impulse,

00:16:19.144 --> 00:16:19.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause it's a
protection, isn't it?

00:16:19.979 --> 00:16:22.014
It's a kinda shield.

00:16:22.014 --> 00:16:24.483
But if everyone was a
bit more courageous...

00:16:24.483 --> 00:16:25.851
When I defend someone's
right to say something

00:16:25.851 --> 00:16:27.586
and I don't like
what they've said,

00:16:27.586 --> 00:16:31.123
invariably I get told that I'm
endorsing what they've said.

00:16:31.123 --> 00:16:33.726 align:start size:88% position:13%
So this is a fundamental
misunderstanding.

00:16:33.726 --> 00:16:35.127
I think generally
even free speech

00:16:35.127 --> 00:16:37.896 align:start position:0%
is a kind of fundamental
misunderstanding of what it is.

00:16:37.896 --> 00:16:40.499 align:start position:0%
Nobody is saying there shouldn't
be consequences to speech.

00:16:40.499 --> 00:16:41.767
Nobody is saying that.

00:16:41.767 --> 00:16:43.836
They're saying that if
you wanna argue back,

00:16:43.836 --> 00:16:46.705
ridicule, whatever,
that's a protesting,

00:16:46.705 --> 00:16:47.673 align:start size:81% position:19%
then that's great.
- Great.

00:16:47.673 --> 00:16:48.941 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I'm all for it, right?
- Yep.

00:16:48.941 --> 00:16:50.509
- It's when you get
the police involved,

00:16:50.509 --> 00:16:53.045 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it's not the same
as perjury or libel laws

00:16:53.045 --> 00:16:55.581 align:start size:84% position:16%
or those kinda
things, or harassment,

00:16:55.581 --> 00:16:57.216
which are already
covered by law.

00:16:58.450 --> 00:16:59.818
And the other one I
get all the time is,

00:16:59.818 --> 00:17:01.720
well, you can't shout
fire in a theater.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:03.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
Actually, you can, but
you're a bit of a dickhead,

00:17:03.922 --> 00:17:07.459
and to be honest, if you do
that, the theater authorities

00:17:07.459 --> 00:17:09.962 align:start size:97% position:3%
are well within their
rights to ask you to leave.

00:17:09.962 --> 00:17:12.531 align:start size:97% position:3%
- There's a little confusion,
some legal confusion

00:17:12.531 --> 00:17:13.899
that has sort of become lore

00:17:13.899 --> 00:17:15.534 align:start size:84% position:16%
about the fire in a
crowded theater thing.

00:17:15.534 --> 00:17:17.970
- It's not true, you can.
- You can't incite a riot,

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:21.106 align:start size:84% position:16%
basically, right,
like there's some...

00:17:22.241 --> 00:17:23.876
- With the incitement
to violence thing,

00:17:23.876 --> 00:17:25.577 align:start size:84% position:16%
I imagine there
are circumstances
where there's a mob

00:17:25.577 --> 00:17:27.112
and someone's talking
to the mob and saying,

00:17:27.112 --> 00:17:29.014
"Go and get that person."
- Well, that you can't do.

00:17:29.014 --> 00:17:30.416
- Right, sure, so we have laws

00:17:30.416 --> 00:17:31.884 align:start size:84% position:16%
that cover incitement
to violence.

00:17:31.884 --> 00:17:34.153 align:start size:88% position:13%
But the trouble is,
this is another example

00:17:34.153 --> 00:17:35.554
of the state getting it wrong.

00:17:35.554 --> 00:17:37.856 align:start size:97% position:3%
So recently we had a
debate in the UK Parliament

00:17:37.856 --> 00:17:39.458
because Boris Johnson
and the Conservatives

00:17:39.458 --> 00:17:41.660 align:start size:88% position:13%
were using language
like the surrender bill

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:44.797 align:start position:0%
to describe a bill that relates
to the Brexit negotiations.

00:17:44.797 --> 00:17:47.199
And they were accused of
using military language

00:17:47.199 --> 00:17:48.967
which incites violence.

00:17:48.967 --> 00:17:50.402
It's called a metaphor, right?

00:17:50.402 --> 00:17:51.603
(Dave laughing)

00:17:51.603 --> 00:17:54.073 align:start size:88% position:13%
Honestly, so many people
on the liberal left

00:17:54.073 --> 00:17:56.508 align:start position:0%
social justice group, they
don't understand basic metaphor.

00:17:56.508 --> 00:17:57.643
They don't understand it.

00:17:57.643 --> 00:17:59.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
They don't understand
jokes, right?

00:17:59.344 --> 00:18:01.280 align:start size:84% position:16%
They take them
completely literally.

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:02.815
There's an example of this,

00:18:02.815 --> 00:18:04.283
there's a comedian called
Andrew Lawrence in the UK

00:18:04.283 --> 00:18:06.652
who posted a joke online, and
I'll say what the joke is,

00:18:06.652 --> 00:18:07.853
it probably is offensive.

00:18:07.853 --> 00:18:08.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
So this was the
joke, so the joke--

00:18:08.854 --> 00:18:10.322
(chair thumping)

00:18:10.322 --> 00:18:11.723 align:start size:97% position:3%
Yeah, brace yourself, right?
- Yeah, okay, I'm ready.

00:18:11.723 --> 00:18:14.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So the joke was basically
that the statistics

00:18:14.093 --> 00:18:17.362
on suicide between men and
women are not comparable,

00:18:17.362 --> 00:18:18.864 align:start size:84% position:16%
more men kill
themselves than women,

00:18:18.864 --> 00:18:20.365
so therefore if feminists
really care about equality,

00:18:20.365 --> 00:18:21.433
they should kill
themselves, right?

00:18:21.433 --> 00:18:22.401
(Dave laughing)

00:18:22.401 --> 00:18:24.236
- That's funny.
- Okay, right.

00:18:24.236 --> 00:18:26.071 align:start position:0%
Now, it's not the joke and
it's not people being offended.

00:18:26.071 --> 00:18:27.806
I think people should be
allowed to be offended,

00:18:27.806 --> 00:18:28.974
and they've got the
right to be offended,

00:18:28.974 --> 00:18:30.242
that's not the issue.

00:18:30.242 --> 00:18:31.743
There was a petition
which called for him

00:18:31.743 --> 00:18:36.148
to be deplatformed from
the BBC, even prosecuted.

00:18:36.148 --> 00:18:38.350
And what they said was
this was inciting women

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:40.085
to kill themselves, right?

00:18:40.085 --> 00:18:42.221
Now, if your opinion
of women is that low,

00:18:42.221 --> 00:18:44.022
that you think they're so stupid

00:18:44.022 --> 00:18:45.524
that there's just
these little drones

00:18:45.524 --> 00:18:47.559
that will kill themselves if
they hear a joke about it,

00:18:47.559 --> 00:18:49.595
and that concerns me, this
kinda literal mindedness

00:18:49.595 --> 00:18:52.464 align:start size:84% position:16%
about metaphor, about
jokes, about art.

00:18:52.464 --> 00:18:53.966 align:start size:88% position:13%
You get that in
Hollywood all the time,

00:18:53.966 --> 00:18:57.002
like we need to represent
women in a certain way,

00:18:57.002 --> 00:18:58.637 align:start size:88% position:13%
so let's make the
"Charlie Angels" reboot,

00:18:58.637 --> 00:18:59.905
which nobody wants to see

00:18:59.905 --> 00:19:01.440
because it's clearly
social engineering,

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:04.776
it's clearly you people,
you think the wrong way,

00:19:04.776 --> 00:19:06.411
and we're the Hollywood people

00:19:06.411 --> 00:19:07.846
and we're gonna tell you
how you should be thinking,

00:19:07.846 --> 00:19:08.680
right?
- Yeah.

00:19:10.015 --> 00:19:11.850 align:start position:0%
- That is rebarbative to
people, it just puts people off.

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.319
- It's so interesting, this
idea that we have to take

00:19:14.319 --> 00:19:16.822 align:start size:88% position:13%
all of these old movies
that were classics,

00:19:16.822 --> 00:19:18.657 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then just remake
them with women,

00:19:18.657 --> 00:19:22.461 align:start size:97% position:3%
as if women can't watch
the old ones or appreciate.

00:19:22.461 --> 00:19:25.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, "Ghostbusters" is
the easiest example of this.

00:19:25.164 --> 00:19:27.566 align:start size:97% position:3%
When they did the all-women
remake, I watched it.

00:19:27.566 --> 00:19:30.636 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have no problem
watching an all-woman cast

00:19:30.636 --> 00:19:32.905
of "Ghostbusters," but the
movie was freaking horrible.

00:19:32.905 --> 00:19:34.940 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, no one cares
about this stuff.

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:36.175
This is the thing about this,

00:19:36.175 --> 00:19:37.910
you don't need to
educate these fans,

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:38.877 align:start size:84% position:16%
particularly science
fiction fans,

00:19:38.877 --> 00:19:40.379
particularly comic book fans.

00:19:40.379 --> 00:19:41.713
They've done this a lot
with changing the genders

00:19:41.713 --> 00:19:43.515
and the race and the
sexual orientation

00:19:43.515 --> 00:19:45.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
of various major
characters, and it's like,

00:19:45.317 --> 00:19:47.452 align:start position:0%
these are already the outsiders,
these comic book fans,

00:19:47.452 --> 00:19:50.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
these are people who
have no issues with this.

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:53.926 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like a school
mistress of finger-pointing

00:19:53.926 --> 00:19:56.795 align:start position:0%
and jabbing at people when
they don't need that education.

00:19:56.795 --> 00:19:57.963 align:start size:81% position:19%
It generates a
lot of resentment.

00:19:57.963 --> 00:19:59.231
People hate it.

00:19:59.231 --> 00:20:00.465
- Do you think it's
also sort of funny,

00:20:00.465 --> 00:20:01.934
I think especially
with the gay thing,

00:20:01.934 --> 00:20:04.970 align:start size:88% position:13%
the way that they sneak
in these messages,

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:06.538
so it's like--
- Oh, come on.

00:20:06.538 --> 00:20:08.040
- The two that I can think of,

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.775
and I'm sure you have
like a gazillion more,

00:20:09.775 --> 00:20:11.276
but the two that
really struck me

00:20:11.276 --> 00:20:14.613
is they're just smacking you
with this in the worst way

00:20:14.613 --> 00:20:18.283 align:start position:0%
is in "Avengers: Endgame,"
they had the one gay character,

00:20:18.283 --> 00:20:20.519
who was just the guy in rehab,

00:20:20.519 --> 00:20:22.588
who I guess maybe went
on a date with a guy,

00:20:22.588 --> 00:20:24.056
and Captain America's
like, "Good for you."

00:20:24.056 --> 00:20:26.825
So that was throwing the
gays like the tiniest bone.

00:20:26.825 --> 00:20:28.060
(Andrew laughing)

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:28.994
No pun intended,
it was just like,

00:20:28.994 --> 00:20:30.229
ah, see, we did something gay.

00:20:30.229 --> 00:20:31.697 align:start size:84% position:16%
That has nothing to
do with the superheros

00:20:31.697 --> 00:20:33.365 align:start size:97% position:3%
or anyone you give a shit
about, but it's something gay.

00:20:33.365 --> 00:20:36.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then the other one was
in "Rise of Skywalker,"

00:20:36.201 --> 00:20:39.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
where at the end that woman
has that lesbian kiss.

00:20:39.104 --> 00:20:40.405
Again, it's not
a real character,

00:20:40.405 --> 00:20:41.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's not anyone
anyone cares about,

00:20:41.673 --> 00:20:43.041 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we did something
for the gays.

00:20:43.041 --> 00:20:45.377 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's just like
take our little pity--

00:20:45.377 --> 00:20:47.946 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, there's that,
but also the gays

00:20:47.946 --> 00:20:50.015 align:start size:97% position:3%
don't need your pity, right?
- Yeah.

00:20:50.015 --> 00:20:51.883
- I don't need to see
pink storm troopers

00:20:51.883 --> 00:20:53.552
and I don't need to see
gay people in "Star Wars"

00:20:53.552 --> 00:20:55.354
for me to feel okay about stuff.

00:20:55.354 --> 00:20:56.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
Anyway, there's been
C-3PO for years.

00:20:56.822 --> 00:20:58.390 align:start size:81% position:19%
There has been
gay representation

00:20:58.390 --> 00:20:59.958
in all of the "Star Wars"
films, it's not an issue.

00:20:59.958 --> 00:21:01.994
- Him and R2-D2 have been in
a robo-sexual relationship.

00:21:01.994 --> 00:21:04.529
- Yeah, quite explicit, and
I don't need to see that.

00:21:04.529 --> 00:21:06.665 align:start size:94% position:6%
They did the same with
"Avengers," like you said,

00:21:06.665 --> 00:21:08.600 align:start size:84% position:16%
they did the same
with the J.K. Rowling,

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:09.901
the "Fantastic Beasts" stuff,

00:21:09.901 --> 00:21:11.937
and they were saying
it wasn't gay enough.

00:21:11.937 --> 00:21:12.871
You see, this is the point.

00:21:12.871 --> 00:21:14.106 line:0%
So you've got Dumbledore,

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:15.574 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
but it's not
explicitly gay enough.

00:21:15.574 --> 00:21:17.442 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
Well, do you wanna see him--
- Do you wanna see him--

00:21:17.442 --> 00:21:18.543 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- I don't know, getting
fisted or something like that?

00:21:18.543 --> 00:21:19.478 line:0%
(Dave laughing)

00:21:19.478 --> 00:21:20.946 line:0%
Is that really what you need?

00:21:20.946 --> 00:21:22.180
Can I say that?

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:23.115
Sorry, I don't know.
- No, you can say it.

00:21:23.115 --> 00:21:24.583
- I mean, I don't need to see--

00:21:24.583 --> 00:21:25.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I don't know that people
wanna hear you say it,

00:21:25.717 --> 00:21:26.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
but you can.
- No, I know, sure.

00:21:26.918 --> 00:21:27.786 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Dumbledore getting
fisted is not...

00:21:27.786 --> 00:21:29.354
(laughing)

00:21:29.354 --> 00:21:31.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I think it's
really patronizing.

00:21:31.356 --> 00:21:32.991
I think ultimately
that's what it is.

00:21:32.991 --> 00:21:35.294
So much of what drives what I do

00:21:35.294 --> 00:21:38.730 align:start size:97% position:3%
is I hate the way these people
patronize minority groups.

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:41.967 align:start size:97% position:3%
I hate that, because I
think that it's the opposite

00:21:41.967 --> 00:21:43.268
of what they say it is, right?

00:21:43.268 --> 00:21:45.537
So if you claim to be
fourth-wave feminism,

00:21:45.537 --> 00:21:47.706
which claims to be all about
empowerment of women, it's not.

00:21:47.706 --> 00:21:49.308
It's about treating
them like children.

00:21:49.308 --> 00:21:50.442
It's about victimizing them.

00:21:50.442 --> 00:21:52.944 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's actually
genuinely misogynistic,

00:21:52.944 --> 00:21:55.914 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it's sort of papered
over with this kind of sheen

00:21:55.914 --> 00:21:57.649 align:start size:84% position:16%
of we're doing
something progressive.

00:21:57.649 --> 00:21:58.950
I don't think there's
anything progressive

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:00.185
about any of this stuff.

00:22:00.185 --> 00:22:01.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the other thing
I hate about it

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:02.554 align:start size:84% position:16%
is I think it
legitimizes bullying.

00:22:02.554 --> 00:22:04.089
I think the social
justice movement...

00:22:04.089 --> 00:22:05.791 align:start size:88% position:13%
I hate bullies, I can't
stand them, right?

00:22:05.791 --> 00:22:07.459
And if you stand
up against them,

00:22:07.459 --> 00:22:08.727 align:start size:81% position:19%
they get even more
vicious, right?

00:22:08.727 --> 00:22:10.162
You must have noticed this.

00:22:10.162 --> 00:22:13.665 align:start position:0%
So it's funny, all of the
signals about how good you are,

00:22:13.665 --> 00:22:16.034
like putting pronouns
in your bio on Twitter

00:22:16.034 --> 00:22:18.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
or putting a rainbow
flag up there on Twitter,

00:22:18.103 --> 00:22:21.807 align:start size:97% position:3%
why is it whenever I get
brutally attacked on Twitter,

00:22:21.807 --> 00:22:23.075
those are the people.

00:22:23.075 --> 00:22:24.443
When I go to their
bio, oh, pronouns.

00:22:24.443 --> 00:22:27.346
And I not saying the
pronouns themselves

00:22:27.346 --> 00:22:29.047 align:start size:88% position:13%
are making a connection
with trans people,

00:22:29.047 --> 00:22:30.415 align:start size:84% position:16%
I want a legitimate
answer to this.

00:22:30.415 --> 00:22:31.750
I've asked this question before.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:33.885 align:start size:84% position:16%
Why is it the case
that the most vicious,

00:22:33.885 --> 00:22:35.987 align:start position:0%
the most bullying, and the
most unpleasant characteristics

00:22:35.987 --> 00:22:37.322
tend to come from those people?

00:22:37.322 --> 00:22:38.290
I don't know why.

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:40.158
- It's so interesting.

00:22:40.158 --> 00:22:41.493
I have some theories as to why,

00:22:41.493 --> 00:22:43.362
which maybe we should
back a little bit,

00:22:43.362 --> 00:22:46.231 align:start size:97% position:3%
but just last week I tweeted
something to that effect.

00:22:46.231 --> 00:22:50.235
Why are the worst people on
Twitter the angriest people?

00:22:50.235 --> 00:22:53.171
It always is the
he/him/they people,

00:22:53.171 --> 00:22:55.240
or they have all the flags
or all the other things.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:58.076
Now, again, I have no problem
with trans people, obviously,

00:22:58.076 --> 00:23:02.347
and I have no problem with
the general idea of justice,

00:23:02.347 --> 00:23:04.182
if you mean equality by justice,

00:23:04.182 --> 00:23:06.184
but why are they all so mean?

00:23:06.184 --> 00:23:07.786
I think the reason for that is,

00:23:07.786 --> 00:23:10.622 align:start position:0%
it has something to do with
personal responsibility, right?

00:23:11.823 --> 00:23:13.992
They've outsourced their
personal responsibility,

00:23:13.992 --> 00:23:15.927
so they don't have to take
care of their own lives.

00:23:15.927 --> 00:23:17.562 align:start size:81% position:19%
It's just be angry
at the system,

00:23:17.562 --> 00:23:20.699 align:start size:94% position:6%
as opposed to sit up
with your shoulders back,

00:23:20.699 --> 00:23:22.067 align:start size:81% position:19%
as that guy said?
- Maybe.

00:23:23.435 --> 00:23:25.003 align:start size:97% position:3%
I imagine a lot of the
people are well-intentioned.

00:23:25.003 --> 00:23:27.706
I imagine if in your mind
you've converted someone

00:23:27.706 --> 00:23:29.141
into this monster, this Nazi,

00:23:29.141 --> 00:23:32.744 align:start size:97% position:3%
then it legitimizes you being
horrible to that person.

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:34.446 align:start size:88% position:13%
But of course the
instinct to be horrible

00:23:34.446 --> 00:23:36.681 align:start size:97% position:3%
to another human being is
an instinct that we all have

00:23:36.681 --> 00:23:38.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we have to keep down
and make sure that...

00:23:38.517 --> 00:23:39.785
That's why we're civilized.

00:23:39.785 --> 00:23:41.720
We're civilized out of
that kind of behavior.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:43.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it worries me that
this kind of thing

00:23:43.822 --> 00:23:47.325
legitimizes that stuff,
and I don't like it.

00:23:47.325 --> 00:23:48.693
But likewise, I don't
wanna demonize people

00:23:48.693 --> 00:23:50.162
